1
00:00:00,516 --> 00:00:01,320
One camera.

2
00:00:01,320 --> 00:00:02,485
A moving viewpoint.

3
00:00:02,485 --> 00:00:04,257
A detailed three dimensional world.

4
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:05,761
This is STREAM SLAM:

5
00:00:06,137 --> 00:00:08,082
sparse tracking and resource efficient

6
00:00:08,082 --> 00:00:09,024
asynchronous mapping

7
00:00:09,024 --> 00:00:10,613
with three dimensional Gaussians.

8
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:13,562
The tracker follows a small set

9
00:00:13,562 --> 00:00:14,655
of image patches.

10
00:00:15,893 --> 00:00:16,956
Their depths anchor

11
00:00:16,956 --> 00:00:18,525
a pretrained completion model,

12
00:00:18,822 --> 00:00:20,125
turning sparse measurements

13
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:21,333
into dense geometry.

14
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:24,713
That geometry supplies the starting points

15
00:00:24,713 --> 00:00:25,839
for our Gaussian map.

16
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:30,420
Completed depth can look smooth

17
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:32,084
and still be unreliable.

18
00:00:32,084 --> 00:00:34,154
We check it against nearby keyframes.

19
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:37,475
Follow this candidate point.

20
00:00:37,475 --> 00:00:39,161
Its depth and the camera poses

21
00:00:39,161 --> 00:00:41,231
project it into the neighboring view.

22
00:00:42,688 --> 00:00:45,117
The projection falls between pixels.

23
00:00:45,117 --> 00:00:46,141
We inspect the four

24
00:00:46,141 --> 00:00:47,691
surrounding depth samples.

25
00:00:49,168 --> 00:00:50,352
Compare these samples

26
00:00:50,352 --> 00:00:51,817
with the projected depth.

27
00:00:51,817 --> 00:00:53,283
A difference below the threshold

28
00:00:53,283 --> 00:00:54,973
gives support from this view.

29
00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:58,894
This second point fails the same check.

30
00:00:58,894 --> 00:01:00,637
We also test the other neighbors

31
00:01:00,637 --> 00:01:02,646
before deciding whether to reject it.

32
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:06,606
No checked view supports this point,

33
00:01:06,606 --> 00:01:07,588
so it is removed.

34
00:01:07,588 --> 00:01:09,176
The supported point is kept.

35
00:01:10,712 --> 00:01:12,576
Repeating this check across the image

36
00:01:12,576 --> 00:01:14,540
filters unsupported geometry

37
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,544
before Gaussian initialization.

38
00:01:16,544 --> 00:01:18,248
This checks depth agreement,

39
00:01:18,248 --> 00:01:19,515
not semantic identity.

40
00:01:23,722 --> 00:01:25,850
This is a real office reconstruction.

41
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:27,435
Camera estimates can change

42
00:01:27,435 --> 00:01:28,559
after parts of the map

43
00:01:28,559 --> 00:01:29,747
have already been built.

44
00:01:31,204 --> 00:01:33,007
The amber Gaussians were created

45
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:34,891
by the highlighted keyframe.

46
00:01:34,891 --> 00:01:36,720
Their source label is retained.

47
00:01:38,156 --> 00:01:40,299
When that camera pose is corrected,

48
00:01:40,299 --> 00:01:41,542
the existing Gaussians

49
00:01:41,542 --> 00:01:43,129
do not move automatically.

50
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:46,430
We apply the same pose correction

51
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:48,776
to their positions and orientations.

52
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:50,200
Each source group follows

53
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:51,187
its own keyframe.

54
00:01:52,643 --> 00:01:54,547
Here we isolate one source group

55
00:01:54,547 --> 00:01:57,332
from a recorded update, replayed slowly.

56
00:01:57,332 --> 00:01:59,056
The surrounding map stays fixed

57
00:01:59,056 --> 00:01:59,802
as a reference.

58
00:02:01,459 --> 00:02:03,189
Now compare two saved runs,

59
00:02:03,506 --> 00:02:04,509
rendered from the same

60
00:02:04,509 --> 00:02:05,939
corrected camera pose.

61
00:02:07,476 --> 00:02:09,600
Without retrospective correction,

62
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,744
the desk and chair boundaries are blurred.

63
00:02:11,744 --> 00:02:12,606
With correction,

64
00:02:12,606 --> 00:02:14,214
their edges are better preserved.

65
00:02:18,189 --> 00:02:19,933
Two processes share the work.

66
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:21,581
The tracker follows the camera,

67
00:02:21,877 --> 00:02:22,920
while the mapper builds

68
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:24,047
the Gaussian scene.

69
00:02:25,504 --> 00:02:27,030
When a keyframe is ready,

70
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:28,939
the tracker sends a numbered task

71
00:02:28,939 --> 00:02:29,606
to the mapper.

72
00:02:31,203 --> 00:02:33,025
Notice that tracking can continue

73
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:34,548
while the mapper is still working

74
00:02:34,548 --> 00:02:35,494
on the first task.

75
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:38,292
The next keyframe,

76
00:02:38,292 --> 00:02:40,035
and then a correction to the first,

77
00:02:40,035 --> 00:02:41,142
join the same queue.

78
00:02:41,437 --> 00:02:42,805
Their order is preserved.

79
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:46,369
But pending work cannot grow forever.

80
00:02:46,665 --> 00:02:48,849
Here, three unconfirmed tasks

81
00:02:48,849 --> 00:02:50,297
fill the illustrated limit.

82
00:02:51,792 --> 00:02:53,556
The fourth task must wait.

83
00:02:53,556 --> 00:02:54,979
Even the task being mapped

84
00:02:54,979 --> 00:02:56,648
still counts toward this limit.

85
00:02:58,183 --> 00:02:59,866
Only after finishing a task

86
00:02:59,866 --> 00:03:02,249
does the mapper send confirmation back.

87
00:03:02,249 --> 00:03:03,551
This frees one place

88
00:03:03,551 --> 00:03:04,598
for the waiting task.

89
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:08,315
The remaining tasks finish in order.

90
00:03:08,315 --> 00:03:10,277
Before closing, the system waits

91
00:03:10,277 --> 00:03:12,384
until every update has been confirmed.

92
00:03:16,010 --> 00:03:18,513
Now, step inside the reconstructed maps.

93
00:03:18,852 --> 00:03:20,962
From furnished rooms and cluttered desks,

94
00:03:21,258 --> 00:03:23,489
to aerial views and outdoor driving,

95
00:03:23,805 --> 00:03:25,409
these are saved reconstructions

96
00:03:25,409 --> 00:03:27,359
from monocular RGB sequences.

97
00:03:27,676 --> 00:03:28,863
The camera paths come

98
00:03:28,863 --> 00:03:30,276
from the estimated poses.

99
00:03:31,486 --> 00:03:32,990
Now compare Splat SLAM,

100
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:35,258
HI SLAM two, and STREAM SLAM.

101
00:03:35,258 --> 00:03:36,682
Watch the enlarged views

102
00:03:36,682 --> 00:03:38,493
for edges and fine textures.

103
00:03:55,576 --> 00:03:57,047
Across eight Replica scenes,

104
00:03:57,384 --> 00:03:58,589
mean trajectory error is

105
00:03:58,589 --> 00:04:00,783
zero point two three six centimeters.

106
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:02,643
Mean PSNR is

107
00:04:02,643 --> 00:04:04,713
thirty nine point six zero decibels,

108
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:06,012
using the paper's

109
00:04:06,012 --> 00:04:07,721
observed view evaluation.

110
00:04:08,955 --> 00:04:10,416
In one common hardware run

111
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:11,702
on Replica office zero,

112
00:04:12,037 --> 00:04:13,298
peak GPU memory is

113
00:04:13,298 --> 00:04:15,005
five point two three gibibytes,

114
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,981
compared with ten point two six

115
00:04:16,981 --> 00:04:18,582
for HI SLAM two.

116
00:04:18,582 --> 00:04:20,467
Processing throughput is similar.

117
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:22,937
These timings exclude scale calibration.

118
00:04:24,075 --> 00:04:25,180
Sparse measurements.

119
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:26,606
Consistent geometry.

120
00:04:26,606 --> 00:04:27,534
Concurrent mapping.

121
00:04:27,951 --> 00:04:29,379
Explore the reconstructions

122
00:04:29,379 --> 00:04:30,645
and camera trajectories

123
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:32,097
on our project page.
